WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.800
- The number one thing
that has made us successful

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.710
by far is obsessive compulsive focus

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.070
on the customer.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:12.750
If you're going to do
anything new or innovative,

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.240
you have to be willing
to be misunderstood.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.740
When you're criticized,
first look in a mirror

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.760
and decide, are your critics right?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.163
If they're right, change.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.340
- Need motivation?

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.600
- Watch your Top 10 with Believe Nation.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.970
- What's up Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.900
I believe in you and
this channel is designed

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.560
to be a part of your daily success routine

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.550
so let's get your motivation to a 10

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.500
and get you believing in you.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.230
Grab a snack and chew on today's lessons

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.740
from a man who went from
quitting his high-paying job

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.340
in New York to driving
across the country to Seattle

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.050
to start his new business,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.670
writing the business plan in
the car ride along the way.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.850
It was called Amazon and it made him

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.210
the richest man in modern times.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.950
He's Jeff Bezos and here is my take

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.640
on his top 10 rules of
success, volume four.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:05.650
Also if you want to know what Jeff

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:06.910
and other successful entrepreneurs

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.440
have to say about building
unstoppable confidence,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.130
check out my 254 confidence series

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.372
where everyday for the next 254 days

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:15.800
I will send you a morning video for free

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.410
to help you build your confidence.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.940
The link to join is in
the description below.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.432
- Stress primarily comes
from not taking action.

00:01:24.432 --> 00:01:27.015
(upbeat music)

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.300
- Okay, let's kick it
off with rule number one.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.540
Focus on your customers.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.100
- The secret sauce of Amazon,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.770
where there are several
principles at Amazon,

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:45.740
but the number one thing that
has made us successful by far

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.540
is obsessive compulsive
focus on the customer

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.480
as opposed to obsession
over the competitor.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.210
And I talk so often to other CEOs and,

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.300
some other CEOs, and also
founders and entrepreneurs,

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.670
and I can tell that even though

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.610
they're talking about customers,

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.230
they're really focusing on competitors,

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.450
and it is a huge advantage to any company

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.900
if you can stay focused on your customer

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.080
instead of your competitor.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.450
- Rule number two, be resourceful.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.450
- I spent an unusual amount
of time with my grandparents,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.840
and especially with my
grandfather on the ranch.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.230
So he had a ranch in South Texas

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.170
and I would spend my summers there

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.320
from age four to 16.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.700
And when I was four, they
were taking me for the summer

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.623
to kind of give my parents a break,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.143
'cause I was so young.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.760
And it was useful.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.853
I was a handful, I'm sure.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:45.923
And anyway,

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.750
he created the illusion for me

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.660
when I was four years old
that I was helping him

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.834
on the ranch, which of course
could not have been true.

00:02:55.834 --> 00:02:56.667
(audience laughs)

00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:57.670
But I believed it.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.610
And by the time I was 16

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.610
of course I was actually
helping on the ranch.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.080
I can prolapsed cattle.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.410
We did all of our own veterinary work.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.529
Some of the cattle even survived.

00:03:10.529 --> 00:03:12.000
(audience laughs)

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.990
And we fixed windmills
and laid water pipelines

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.810
and built fences and barns

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.530
and fixed the bulldozer
that you guys talked about.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.060
And so one of the things
that's so interesting

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.700
about that lifestyle
and about my grandfather

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.290
is he did everything himself.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.850
He didn't call a vet if one
of the animals was sick.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.223
He figured out what to do himself.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:37.860
- So what does it mean, no delegation?

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.583
- Being resourceful I think.

00:03:42.163 --> 00:03:44.890
If there's a problem, there's a solution.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.600
And of course as you mature
and get into the business world

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:50.970
and anything you do on a team

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.968
you very quickly realize
that it's not about

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:55.120
just your own resourcefulness,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.530
it's about team resourcefulness
and how does that work.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.010
- Rule number three, love what you do.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.210
- I have this great luxury, I love my job.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.270
I tap dance into work.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.923
I just got back from an
amazing vacation in Norway.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:14.120
I got to go dog sledding
and go to a wolf preserve

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.680
and all this really cool stuff,

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.720
but I couldn't wait to get back
to work 'cause it's so fun.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.500
And one of the reasons it's fun for me

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.050
is I get to work in the future,

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.880
so my job, I have very

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.870
limited day-to-day operational needs.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:35.880
I've constructed my job

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.120
so that I don't have to be
pulled into the present.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.480
I can stay two or three
years in the future.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.140
- Rule number four, start small.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.700
- Everything I have ever
done has started small.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:54.340
Amazon started with a
couple of people, and

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:59.480
Blue Origin started with five people.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.220
And the budget at Blue
Origin was very, very small.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.830
Now the Blue Origin approaches
a billion dollars a year,

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.800
and next year it'll be more
than a billion dollars.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.460
And Amazon, it literally was 10 people.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.100
Today it's half a million people,

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.260
but it's hard to remember for you guys,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.500
but for me it's like yesterday
I was driving the packages

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:17.837
to the Post Office myself

00:05:17.837 --> 00:05:21.703
and hoping one day we
could afford a forklift.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.770
So for me, I've seen small things get big

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.230
and it's part of this day one mentality.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.016
I like treating things
as if, if they're small,

00:05:31.016 --> 00:05:34.350
Amazon even though it is a large company,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.950
I want it to have the heart
and spirit of a small one.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:40.100
- Rule number five, learn
how to deal with critics.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.490
- My approach to criticism

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.420
and what I teach and preach inside Amazon

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:48.900
is when you're criticized,
first look in a mirror

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.605
and decide, are your critics right?

00:05:51.605 --> 00:05:54.250
If they're right, change.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.876
There are two kinds of critics.

00:05:56.876 --> 00:06:00.733
There are well-meaning critics who,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.210
they're worried it's not going to work

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:06.810
but they do want it to work,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.790
and I could give you an
example of customer reviews

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.490
would be one of those.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.933
When we first did customer
reviews 20 years ago,

00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:17.680
some book publishers
were not happy about it

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.410
because some of them were negative,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.680
and so it was a very controversial
practice at that time,

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:22.740
but we thought it was right

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.700
and so we stuck to our guns
and had a deep heel on that

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.533
and didn't change.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.480
But there's a second kind of critic,

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.150
which is the self-interested critic,

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.050
and they come in all shapes and sizes.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.090
So they can be any kind of institution,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.140
competitors of course,

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.280
and so when you are doing
something in a new way,

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.430
and if customers embrace the new way,

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.940
what's going to happen is incumbents

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.410
who are practicing the older
way are not going to like you,

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.430
and they're going to be
self-interested critics.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:00.550
And so you do need as you're
looking yourself in the mirror

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.000
to try and tease those two things apart.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.550
- Rule number six, be effective.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.140
- We try to

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.060
create teams that are no larger

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:13.560
than can be fed with two pizzas.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.310
We call that the two pizza team rule.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.050
No PowerPoints are used inside of Amazon.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.390
So every meeting, when
we hire a new executive

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:24.223
from the outside,

00:07:24.223 --> 00:07:26.820
(audience laughs)

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.290
this is the weirdest meeting culture

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:32.080
you will ever encounter,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.080
and new executives have a
little bit of culture shock

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:38.790
in their first Amazon meeting

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:42.160
because what we do is
somebody for the meeting

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.000
has prepared a six page memo,

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.540
a narratively structured memo

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.070
that has got real sentences
and topic sentences

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:54.250
and verbs and nouns,
not just bullet points.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:57.600
And it lays out and supposed
to create the context

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.237
for what will then be a good discussion.

00:08:00.237 --> 00:08:05.237
And then we read those memos
silently in the meeting,

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.900
so it's like a study hall.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.640
And we do that, everybody
sits around the table

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.310
and we read silently for
usually about half an hour,

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.410
however long it takes
us to read the document,

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.230
and then we discuss it.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.010
And it's so much better

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:25.290
than the typical PowerPoint presentation

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.460
for so many reasons.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.090
I definitely recommend the
memo over the PowerPoint,

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.920
and the reason we read them
in the room by the way,

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.140
is because just like high school kids,

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.150
executives will bluff their
way through the meeting

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.547
as if they've read the memo,

00:08:40.547 --> 00:08:41.570
(audience laughs)

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:45.920
because we're busy and
so you got to actually

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:48.520
carve out the time for
the memo to get read,

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.640
and that's what the first half
hour of the meeting is for,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.950
and then everybody's
actually read the memo.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.410
They're not just pretending
to have read the memo.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.020
- Rule number seven,
follow your intuition.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.430
- I believe in the power of wandering.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:04.740
All of my best decisions
in business and in life

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:09.740
have been made with heart, intuition, guts

00:09:13.699 --> 00:09:17.000
not analysis.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.310
When you can make a decision
with analysis, you should do so

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.470
but it turns out in life

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:23.190
that you're most important decisions

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:27.570
are always made with instinct,
intuition, taste, heart.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.770
- Rule number eight,
act on your great ideas.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:34.430
- I picked books because
there were more items

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.490
in the book category
than any other category,

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:39.270
and so you could build
universal selection.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:40.130
There were three million,

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:44.363
in 1994 when I was pulling
this idea together,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.520
three million different books

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.160
active and in print at any given time

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:50.700
and the largest physical bookstores

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.570
only had about 150,000 different titles,

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:56.220
and so I could see how you
could make a bookstore online

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:57.840
with universal selection.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.400
Every book ever printed,
even the out of print ones

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.980
was the original vision for the company,

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:04.220
and so that's why books.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.100
- And when did you know that Amazon

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.840
is going to be something way
bigger than just a bookstore?

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.430
- Well I knew that the books,

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.620
strangely 'cause I was very prepared

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:14.990
for this to take a really long time.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:18.750
I knew that the books business
was going to be successful

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.000
in the first 30 days.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.010
I was shocked at how many books we sold.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.803
We were ill-prepared.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.870
We had only 10 people in
the company at that time

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:31.420
and most of them were software engineers,

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.263
and so everybody, including
me and the softwares,

00:10:33.263 --> 00:10:35.530
were all packing boxes.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:38.000
We didn't even have packing tables.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.110
We were on our hands and
knees on a concrete floor

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.620
packing the boxes,

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:43.720
and about one or two in the morning

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:48.160
I said to one of my software
engineering colleagues,

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.853
I said, "Paul,

00:10:52.759 --> 00:10:54.327
"this is killing my knees.

00:10:54.327 --> 00:10:56.370
"We need to get knee pads."

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:57.607
And Paul looked at me and he's like,

00:10:57.607 --> 00:11:00.364
"Jeff, we need to get packing tables."

00:11:00.364 --> 00:11:02.008
(audience laughs)

00:11:02.008 --> 00:11:05.780
And I was like, "Oh my God,
that is such a good idea."

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.397
The next day I bought packing tables

00:11:07.397 --> 00:11:09.560
and it doubled our productivity

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:12.100
and probably saved our
backs and our knees too.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.610
- Rule number nine, be a visionary.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.630
- My greatest, I would
have such a good feeling

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.240
if I could be an 80-year-old guy

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.590
and laying there thinking about my life

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:27.010
if I could say look, there is
now a bunch of entrepreneurs

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:30.550
in space because I took
my Amazon lottery winnings

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:32.840
and built the heavy-lifting
infrastructure,

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:36.000
that does take billions
of dollars in cap ex,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.760
to lower the cost of access to space.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.290
That's how you get millions of people

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:42.123
living and working in space.

00:11:42.123 --> 00:11:43.540
And by the way, we need that.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:48.260
For those of you who like to
think about the future at all,

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:50.290
you can do a simple calculation.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:53.773
We can argue about what
limited resources on Earth

00:11:53.773 --> 00:11:55.100
and so on and so on,

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.439
but here's the calculation
that you cannot argue with,

00:11:57.439 --> 00:12:01.730
which is you take current
baseline energy usage on Earth,

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:04.680
compound it at just a few percent a year

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.960
for just a few hundred years,

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.370
and you have to cover the
entire surface of the Earth

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.100
in solar cells, so

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.870
we're going to have to decide

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:20.870
do we want a society of
pioneering, invention,

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.150
expansion, growth?

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.380
Or do we want a society of stasis?

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:28.850
And personally, because
the Earth is finite,

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:31.370
and if you want a society of stasis,

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:32.203
I think it's got,

00:12:32.203 --> 00:12:33.490
first of all, I don't personally believe

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.780
that stasis is even
compatible with freedom,

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.970
so I think for me that's a big problem.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:41.580
Second of all, it's going to be dull.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.210
Stasis is going to be very dull.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.300
You don't want to live
in the stasis world.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:46.960
And so, of course we're going to continue

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:47.940
to get more efficient too.

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:49.680
We have been for hundreds of years,

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.760
we've been getting more
productive, more efficient.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.300
That trend is going to continue.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.070
But even so, we're going
to want to use more energy,

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.450
more energy per capita,

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:00.740
and also, I don't want
to stabilize population.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.470
I would love for there
to be a trillion humans

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:03.690
in the solar system.

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:07.220
With a trillion humans, we
would have 1,000 Einsteins

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:08.710
and 1,000 Mozarts.

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:11.920
It'd be an incredible, don't
you want that dynamism?

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:13.310
It'd be so much more interesting.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:15.380
This is for your great,
great, great, grandchildren,

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.580
but what kind of world do
you want them to live in?

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.170
I want them to live in
that expansive world

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:23.870
that is learning more about the universe

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:25.670
and moving out throughout
the solar system.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:27.040
- And rule number ten, the last one

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.589
before a very special bonus clip,

00:13:28.589 --> 00:13:31.700
is be willing to be misunderstood.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:36.240
- If you're going to do
anything new or innovative,

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.750
you have to be willing
to be misunderstood.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:40.730
And if you can't tolerate that,

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:43.763
then for God's sake, don't do
anything new or innovative.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.880
Every important thing we've done

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.710
has been misunderstood

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:54.350
often by well-meaning, sincere critics.

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:59.350
Sometimes, of course, by
self-interested, insincere critics.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.240
